<BaseLayout title="About — ACES Technology" description="About Joshua Parr and ACES Technology.">
  <section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>Independent research, grounded in hands-on engineering.</h1></section>
  <section class="two-column">
    <div class="prose"><h2>Joshua Parr</h2><p>Joshua is an independent researcher with an associate degree in engineering and three completed years of electrical-engineering study at the University of Virginia. He intends to return to complete his final undergraduate year when circumstances permit.</p><p>His work grows from more than a decade of self-directed study in combat-aircraft technology and from hands-on development with embedded computers, sensors, communications hardware, and mobile research platforms.</p><p>ACES Technology is the working identity for that research: a place to document prototypes, publish technical analysis, and make the engineering process visible to potential collaborators in academia, government, and industry.</p><h2>Research approach</h2><p>The work deliberately separates demonstrated results from planned capability. Small-scale prototypes investigate integration problems that also matter in larger systems: interface stability, component replacement, data normalization, power integrity, verification, and human-readable technical evidence.</p></div>
    <aside class="fact-panel"><p class="eyebrow">Research profile</p><ul><li>Electrical engineering</li><li>Embedded hardware and software</li><li>Modular sensor integration</li><li>Aerospace and defense systems</li><li>Open-systems architecture</li><li>Human-machine teaming</li></ul><p class="panel-note">Charlottesville, Virginia</p></aside>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
